        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-8b4ecac624eee413\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Armand’s “je vous aime trop” is principally an avowal presented as true and used to explain his jealousy and distress. It neither undertakes a commitment nor shows positive evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge; its emotional intensity is adequately treated as context for a T utterance.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Armand speaks the clause directly to Marguerite within the novel’s represented dialogue.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs during reconciliation after Armand has suspiciously challenged Marguerite’s secret plan. The modifier “trop” and the following account of jealousy frame his love as excessive and as the cause of his suspicion and heartache. His narration of what he is prepared to suffer and his subsequent willingness to obey support the seriousness of the avowal, but the core words themselves do not enact a distinct undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Que voulez-vous, Marguerite, je vous aime trop, et je suis jaloux de la moindre de vos pensées.”\",\"supports\":\"The love clause is a direct first-person avowal, and its coordination with jealousy presents love as an existing emotional state explaining Armand’s conduct.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ce que vous m'avez proposé tout à l'heure me rendrait fou de joie, mais le mystère qui précède l'exécution de ce projet me serre le cœur.”\",\"supports\":\"The continuation elaborates Armand’s emotionally conflicted state, reinforcing T while supplying no independent commitment or reflex-like force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Armand asks Marguerite’s forgiveness after accusing her of involving the comte de G. in her plan.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal functions as an explanation and reassurance within a reconciliation, rather than as a new relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says he watched Marguerite “avec amour” while thinking about what he was ready to suffer for her; later he says he would have obeyed even a request to commit a crime.\",\"supports\":\"The broader scene supports the represented depth and sincerity of Armand’s loving state, without changing the core utterance from an avowal into a performative commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense “je vous aime” in Chapter XIII of Alexandre Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical and bibliographic identification of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its explanatory, jealous, and emotionally intense framing is contextual and creates no residual pressure requiring E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
